diff --git a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
index 7d1a5a4f8..1523cccbe 100644
--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
@@ -156,6 +156,7 @@ Some features are not supported in AdvCiv-SAS; e.g., non-English languages, pre-
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6022+) **SAS game-summary log:** added a separate `SASGameSummary_*.log` for compact autoplay and AI-strength review. It records high-level run context such as economy, expansion, city and battle history, worked plots, diplomacy, unit composition, game state, and in-game bonus appearances/disappearances, complementing BBAI logs by summarizing what happened before or alongside detailed AI-decision traces. Note: it currently contains spoilers: a spoiler-free implementation was considered, so the human/active player can give to an LLM or such their play summary and get LLM or such suggestions or feedback/analysis, but it would require an extensive rework, so currently this is not supported; therefore, SAS game-summary log should as of now not be treated as a spoiler-free player-advice export. Note 2: depending on `SAS_GAME_SUMMARY_TURN_INTERVAL` value, the last turn may not appear in the log: for example, an autoplay run of 200 turns ends at T200 and thus does not seem to show the T200 data, only up to T190, so autoplay at least 201 turns to show T200 for example if you have this issue. See also [README.md (DLL Logging)](/README.md#dll-logging).
 - **BBAI worker log split:** added separate worker and Work Boat / `UNITAI_WORKER_SEA` BBAI log-level defines so ordinary `SAS_BBAI_UNIT_LOG_LEVEL` can focus on non-worker unit behavior such as scrapping, army movement, and combat-unit decisions. Worker and worker-sea logs had become noisy enough to hide those unit decisions, and Work Boats use distinct logic from land workers, so they now have their own category as well.
 - **BBAI Settler log split:** added `SAS_BBAI_SETTLER_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT so Settlers and other units can be inspected independently. This notably covers city-site decisions, Settler movement, scouting, returns, founding, (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) parking reasons, danger context, and city escort pools; FOUND as of now separately controls detailed city-site scoring calculations. This notably lets UNIT investigate other unit behavior without enabling the multi-megabyte first-city Settler traces needed for example for the Berlin, Karakorum, and Cuzco regression tests.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6031+) **Great General BBAI diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_GREAT_GENERAL_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT notably so Military Academy construction, Military Instructor joining, Warlord attachment, target-city choice, and target-unit choice can be inspected together without enabling broad unit logs. This notably helped isolate a bad France sample where `AI_join` rejected all cities for safety/path reasons, then the plot-danger branch attached a Great General to a low-value Longbowman.
 - **Culture-victory BBAI diagnostics:** added a new dedicated `SAS_BBAI_CULTURE_LOG_LEVEL` notably for diagnosing premature, uneven, or wasteful AI culture investment without enabling broad player/city logs. It records for example culture-stage reasons, current/projected Legendary-city countdowns and rival ranking, competing victory progress, actual culture-slider decisions, per-city weights and production, and culture contributed by production processes and assigned specialists.
 - **Citizen-allocation BBAI diagnostics:** added a dedicated `SAS_BBAI_CITIZEN_LOG_LEVEL` notably for finding worked plots or specialists that the AI's full city-aware comparison would replace, but its faster preliminary selection overlooked. At the highest level, it also reports suspicious worked/unworked plot pairs under a simple food-first yield check.
 - **Evacuation and retreat BBAI diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_EVACUATION_LOG_LEVEL`. It notably helps investigate evacuation/retreat behavior without broad city or unit logging, for doomed cities, workers, and naval units.

